# org.env — per-organisation config for a worldmonitor-local install.
#
# The downloadable bundle is org-neutral: it carries no Supabase or Upstash
# values. Fill this in with your organisation's values, then either:
#   - save it next to install.sh as `org.env`, or
#   - pass it:  ./install.sh --config /path/to/org.env   (install.ps1 -Config on Windows)
#
# install.sh copies these into the generated .env and adds
# LOCAL_API_MODE=tauri-sidecar. Only KEY=value lines; # comments and blanks are
# ignored. VITE_SUPABASE_URL is mirrored to SUPABASE_URL automatically.

# ── required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your Supabase project. Both are public by design (the anon key ships in a
# normally-built web bundle). The standalone backend injects these into the
# dashboard HTML at request time as window.__WM_RUNTIME_CONFIG.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=YOUR-PUBLISHABLE-OR-ANON-KEY

# ── optional: self-refreshing local cache ───────────────────────────────
# READ-ONLY token only. A local instance never needs — and must never hold —
# a write credential to the shared store. Without these the backend still
# serves whatever is already cached; nothing refreshes it.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://YOUR-DB.upstash.io
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_READONLY_TOKEN=YOUR-READ-ONLY-TOKEN

# ── optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your deployment's domain (cosmetic for a loopback dashboard — affects
# canonical URLs and Sentry environment detection only).
# APP_DOMAIN=worldmonitor.example

# AI summary panels — each operator adds their OWN OpenRouter key to .env
# after install; it is deliberately NOT collected here.
